{"slug":"urea","name":"Urea / Ammonium Sulphate","chemicalFormula":"CH₄N₂O / (NH₄)₂SO₄","purpose":"Added illegally to artificially boost non-protein nitrogen, falsifying SNF (Solids-Not-Fat) and protein readings.","snfImpact":"Artificially elevates calculated SNF readings by up to 2.5% to deceive standard refractometer and lactometer checks.","densityImpact":"Slightly increases specific gravity to conceal heavy water dilution.","chemicalMechanism":"p-Dimethylaminobenzaldehyde (DMAB) condensation reaction in acidic media forming a distinctive lemon-yellow chromogen.","healthHazards":["Severe nephrotoxicity and progressive chronic kidney failure","Gastric ulceration and irritation of intestinal mucosa","Metabolic acidosis and cellular dehydration","Forms carcinogenic nitrosamines when combined with nitrate contaminants"],"fssaiLimit":"Max permissible natural urea in milk: 700 ppm (70 mg/100ml). Any synthetic addition is strictly illegal.","referenceImage":"/references/urea_reference.png","pureStripImage":"/references/urea_pure.png","adulteratedStripImage":"/references/urea_adulterated.png","pureColor":{"hex":"#B6B3A4","rgb":[182,179,164],"name":"Pale Control Pad (Off-White)","description":"Left pad: normal pure milk control pad from calibrated reference."},"adulteratedColor":{"hex":"#B0B63B","rgb":[176,182,59],"name":"Vibrant Lemon-Yellow","description":"Right pad: turns vivid lemon-yellow in presence of synthetic urea/ammonium."},"detectionTips":["Left pad = normal/pure off-white; Right pad = adulterated lemon-yellow.","Yellow saturation directly correlates with synthetic urea concentration.","Allow strip reaction time of 60 seconds at room temperature."],"_id":"5rIO5q8AxYyDcU94"}
{"slug":"starch","name":"Starch / Maltodextrin","chemicalFormula":"(C₆H₁₀O₅)ₙ","purpose":"Used to thicken diluted milk, restoring viscosity, creaminess, and lactometer density after water dilution.","snfImpact":"Falsely restores total solid readings by adding complex carbohydrates instead of natural milk fat and proteins.","densityImpact":"Increases specific gravity from watered readings (<1.026) back into the normal range (1.028–1.032 g/cm³).","chemicalMechanism":"Iodine-starch inclusion reaction: Triiodide (I₃⁻) ions insert into the amylose helix, creating an intense dark blue-black charge transfer complex.","healthHazards":["Rapid blood glucose spikes, dangerous for diabetic individuals","Severe indigestion, diarrhea, and colic in infants","Prevents absorption of natural calcium and casein proteins in the gastrointestinal tract","Promotes rapid microbial fermentation and souring"],"fssaiLimit":"Nil tolerance (0.0%). Any presence of external starch or maltodextrin indicates adulteration.","referenceImage":"/references/starch_reference.jpg","pureStripImage":"/references/starch_pure.png","adulteratedStripImage":"/references/starch_adulterated.png","pureColor":{"hex":"#594214","rgb":[89,66,20],"name":"Amber Mustard Brown","description":"Left pad: normal pure milk reaction pad from calibrated reference."},"adulteratedColor":{"hex":"#120F0B","rgb":[18,15,11],"name":"Deep Blue-Black / Pitch Charcoal","description":"Right pad: turns intensely dark blue-black / charcoal complex with amylose."},"detectionTips":["Left pad = normal/amber-brown; Right pad = adulterated deep charcoal/black.","Boiling milk beforehand can increase starch gelatinization sensitivity.","Even trace quantities of maltodextrin trigger noticeable darkening."],"_id":"gvgCNP14WhhCzbkf"}
{"slug":"detergent","name":"Detergent / Commercial Soap","chemicalFormula":"C₁₂H₂₅SO₄Na (Anionic Surfactants)","purpose":"Added to create artificial dense froth, emulsify cheap vegetable oils with water, and produce synthetic milk.","snfImpact":"Distorts standard colloidal balance and alters fat globule dispersion properties.","densityImpact":"Maintains uniform suspension of adulterated fats without spontaneous phase separation.","chemicalMechanism":"Anionic surfactant displacement reaction with cationic dye (e.g., bromocresol purple or pinacyanol) resulting in a dark slate-blue precipitation.","healthHazards":["Corrosive injury to esophagus, stomach lining, and intestines","Acute alkaline gastroenteritis and persistent vomiting","Alkaline poisoning causing disruption of systemic enzyme pathways","Chronic toxic stress on liver filtration systems"],"fssaiLimit":"Zero tolerance (0.0%). Absolutely prohibited in food products.","referenceImage":"/references/detergent_reference.png","pureStripImage":"/references/detergent_pure.png","adulteratedStripImage":"/references/detergent_adulterated.png","pureColor":{"hex":"#856E72","rgb":[133,110,114],"name":"Mauve / Soft Lavender Pink","description":"Left pad: normal pure milk control pad from calibrated reference."},"adulteratedColor":{"hex":"#3A3B39","rgb":[58,59,57],"name":"Dark Slate Charcoal","description":"Right pad: shifts to dark slate-blue / charcoal with surfactant."},"detectionTips":["Left pad = normal mauve/pink-lavender; Right pad = adulterated dark slate charcoal.","Lathering test: vigorous shaking produces persistent dense foam that stays for >10 mins.","Strip detection responds quickly within 30 to 45 seconds."],"_id":"o6eaaxjp6GDGrYmn"}
{"slug":"formalin","name":"Formalin (Formaldehyde)","chemicalFormula":"HCHO / CH₂O","purpose":"Illegally used as a preservative to prevent bacterial spoilage and extend shelf life of adulterated or stale milk for days without refrigeration.","snfImpact":"Does not directly affect SNF readings; masks microbial activity that would otherwise cause souring and curdling detectable by standard tests.","densityImpact":"Minimal effect on density; makes stale milk appear fresh by inhibiting fermentation-driven density changes.","chemicalMechanism":"Schiff reagent (fuchsin-sulfurous acid) or KMnO₄ decolorization reaction: formaldehyde oxidizes the reagent producing a vivid magenta-crimson to deep violet chromophore complex. In the absence of formalin, strips remain off-white or pale.","healthHazards":["Classified as a Group 1 human carcinogen (IARC) — directly linked to nasopharyngeal and leukemia cancers","Acute oral toxicity: corrosive necrosis of esophagus, stomach mucosa, and intestinal lining","Severe hepatotoxicity and irreversible liver cirrhosis with chronic exposure","Respiratory damage, chronic bronchitis, and mucosal inflammation","Systemic neurotoxicity with memory impairment, motor dysfunction, and CNS depression"],"fssaiLimit":"Absolute zero tolerance (0.0 ppm). Formalin is strictly banned under FSSAI Food Safety & Standards (Contaminants, Toxins and Residues) Regulations.","referenceImage":"/references/formalin_reference.png","pureStripImage":"/references/formalin_pure.png","adulteratedStripImage":"/references/formalin_adulterated.png","pureColor":{"hex":"#5A051E","rgb":[90,5,30],"name":"Crimson-Magenta","description":"Formalin-free (pure milk) left/top pad on Schiff/KMnO₄ strip."},"adulteratedColor":{"hex":"#3E1A58","rgb":[62,26,88],"name":"Deep Violet-Purple","description":"Formaldehyde reacts with Schiff reagent producing a deep violet-purple shift on the right/bottom pad."},"detectionTips":["Left/top pad is the normal reference (crimson); right/bottom pad is adulterated (violet).","Even trace formalin causes a noticeable shift towards violet-purple.","Strip reaction is rapid — results visible within 20 to 40 seconds at room temperature."],"_id":"xXwGyQaVeIgOa9nU"}
{"slug":"formalin","name":"Formalin (Formaldehyde)","chemicalFormula":"HCHO / CH₂O","purpose":"Illegally used as a preservative to prevent bacterial spoilage and extend shelf life of adulterated or stale milk for days without refrigeration.","snfImpact":"Does not directly affect SNF readings; masks microbial activity that would otherwise cause souring and curdling detectable by standard tests.","densityImpact":"Minimal effect on density; makes stale milk appear fresh by inhibiting fermentation-driven density changes.","chemicalMechanism":"Schiff reagent (fuchsin-sulfurous acid) or KMnO₄ decolorization reaction: formaldehyde oxidizes the reagent producing a vivid magenta-crimson to deep violet chromophore complex. In the absence of formalin, strips remain off-white or pale.","healthHazards":["Classified as a Group 1 human carcinogen (IARC) — directly linked to nasopharyngeal and leukemia cancers","Acute oral toxicity: corrosive necrosis of esophagus, stomach mucosa, and intestinal lining","Severe hepatotoxicity and irreversible liver cirrhosis with chronic exposure","Respiratory damage, chronic bronchitis, and mucosal inflammation","Systemic neurotoxicity with memory impairment, motor dysfunction, and CNS depression"],"fssaiLimit":"Absolute zero tolerance (0.0 ppm). Formalin is strictly banned under FSSAI Food Safety & Standards (Contaminants, Toxins and Residues) Regulations.","referenceImage":"/references/formalin_reference.png","pureStripImage":"/references/formalin_pure.png","adulteratedStripImage":"/references/formalin_adulterated.png","pureColor":{"hex":"#5A051E","rgb":[90,5,30],"name":"Crimson-Magenta","description":"Formalin-free (pure milk) left/top pad on Schiff/KMnO₄ strip."},"adulteratedColor":{"hex":"#3E1A58","rgb":[62,26,88],"name":"Deep Violet-Purple","description":"Formaldehyde reacts with Schiff reagent producing a deep violet-purple shift on the right/bottom pad."},"detectionTips":["Left/top pad is the normal reference (crimson); right/bottom pad is adulterated (violet).","Even trace formalin causes a noticeable shift towards violet-purple.","Strip reaction is rapid — results visible within 20 to 40 seconds at room temperature."],"_id":"xXwGyQaVeIgOa9nU"}
